<!DOCTYPE qhelp PUBLIC "-//Semmle//qhelp//EN" "qhelp.dtd">
<qhelp>
<overview>
<p>
Python's <code>random</code> module (including <code>random.Random</code> instances) uses the
Mersenne Twister pseudo-random number generator. It is not cryptographically secure: an attacker
who observes enough outputs can recover its internal state and predict past and future values.
Using it to generate API keys, session identifiers, tokens, passwords, nonces, salts or keys
allows an attacker to guess those secrets.
</p>
</overview>

<recommendation>
<p>
Use the <code>secrets</code> module (for example <code>secrets.token_hex</code>,
<code>secrets.token_urlsafe</code> or <code>secrets.choice</code>), <code>os.urandom</code>, or
<code>random.SystemRandom</code> for any security-sensitive value.
</p>
</recommendation>

<example>
<p>
In the following example an API key is derived from <code>random.getrandbits</code>. Hashing the
value does not help, because the hash input is predictable:
</p>
<sample src="WeakCryptoRandomnessBad.py" />
<p>
The fix is to use the <code>secrets</code> module:
